&lt;div&gt;
 Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly expanding across sectors, but the computational infrastructure supporting advanced AI systems is increasingly associated with significant energy demand and environmental impacts. According to the International Energy Agency, global data centres consumed roughly 460 terawatt-hours (TWh) of electricity in 2022, representing about 2% of global electricity demand, with AI-driven workloads expected to increase this demand further in the coming decade. As countries accelerate investments in AI capabilities, integrating environmental sustainability into AI governance has become an emerging policy challenge.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 India has recently intensified its efforts to expand its AI ecosystem through initiatives such as the IndiaAI Mission, which aims to strengthen national computing capacity and support AI innovation. However, India’s current AI governance approach remains largely principle-based and advisory, with limited mechanisms addressing the environmental footprint of AI systems and data-centre infrastructure. Existing environmental regulations—such as the Environment Protection Act, 1986 and the Energy Conservation Act, 2001—do not explicitly account for the energy intensity and resource demands associated with large-scale AI development.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 This policy brief examines the governance gap between rapid AI expansion and environmental oversight in India and compares it with emerging international regulatory approaches, including the EU Artificial Intelligence Act. It proposes policy measures for integrating Green AI governance in India, including environmental disclosure requirements, sustainability standards for AI infrastructure, and incentives for energy-efficient AI development. Such measures can help ensure that technological advancement aligns with long-term environmental sustainability.
&lt;/div&gt;
